            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we had identified a few air leakage locations that you will want to take care of:



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The location in the 2nd floor bathroom ceiling where the bathroom fan was previously located



** The bathroom fan in that 2nd floor bathroom ceiling allows a lot of leakage &amp; could be upgraded to a new Energy Star rated unit with a good internal butterfly damper



** An intentional hole is the 6" chimney for the current furnace (that could be eliminated with a furnace upgrade)



Savings: For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, and you could potentially save $ 82 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ</airSeal>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1,185 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house has an older 83.4% efficient oil, vented up a 6" chimney. As you consider a future upgrade, I modeled the option to install an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Also look at my "Additional Comments" section regarding the "Depressurization Test, Combustion Air Supply &amp; Fresh Air Supply".



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 634 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test, Combustion Air Supply &amp; Fresh Air Supply



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the one bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent - your current furnace &amp; wood stove are both chimney vented.



Your house has just about the right balance of ventilation, and is close to achieving the 5 Pascals threshold of when combustion gases could be back-drafted into the house. As discussed, you should consider installing a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe, directly connected to the back of your wood stove.



If you upgrade the furnace to a high efficiency propane unit, the current furnace chimney will be eliminated. There is a 5" outside fresh air supply connected to the return air ductwork of the furnace. I mentioned that you should have an adjustable damper installed in that pipe so you can control the amount of fresh air entering the home. If the house feels dry in the winter, partially close the damper. If you have excess condensation buildup on your windows, than open the damper further to achieve the balance you desire.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - A clothes dryer



If you decide to replace the bathroom fan, please select an Energy Star rated unit. You may want to consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



